#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: ggdef
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [cow, borrow, copy]
# doc: |
#   RV-F #11 (Copy axis, ACCEPT direction — the STRUCT extension pin). `h.p` is
#   an all-int STRUCT field (`Point {int a; int b}`), passed bare alongside the
#   exclusive `&h` in the same call. An all-Copy-field struct is itself Copy, so
#   the bare read is a value snapshot exempt from D10(b) place-overlap — accepted,
#   printing `h.n = 1 + 1 + 2 = 4`. THIS fixture is the pin on the #11 divergence:
#   the pre-fix oracle's Prim-only Copy axis rejected exactly this shape with
#   E_BorrowConflict (counterfactual-verified — reverting ty_is_copy to Prim-only
#   turns this fixture red) while production accepts it via is_copy_type's
#   struct recursion. Its scalar sibling copy_field_borrow_ok.gg (an int field)
#   was accepted by BOTH the old and new axes and guards only the scalar base
#   case.
#
#   KNOWN SELF-HOST GAP (filed — the RV-D cluster's "self-host twin of RV-F's
#   Copy-axis narrowing", TODO.md): the self-host safety walk's Copy axis is
#   scalar-only, so its D10(b) mirror REJECTS this program (E_BorrowConflict at
#   the driver) — the self-host lane BUILD-FAILs this fixture BY DESIGN until the
#   RV-D Copy-axis twin lands and flips it to four-lane MATCH. The expected
#   verdict here is what the language SHOULD do (accept), never a ratification of
#   the narrow axis — same staging pattern as the four single-owner-Callable
#   rejects.
# expect:
#   exit: 0
#   stdout: "4\n"
#!end

struct Point:
    int a
    int b

struct Holder:
    Point p
    int n

void bump(Holder &h, Point pt):
    h.n = h.n + pt.a + pt.b

void main():
    Holder h = Holder(Point(1, 2), 1)
    bump(&h, h.p)
    print(h.n)
